[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Selectors and Pseudo-classes (:is, :where, :has) in CSS3"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern CSS has introduced powerful relational and functional pseudo-classes that dramatically reduce repetition and increase stylesheet readability. Among the most transformative additions are :is(), :where(), and :has()."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Functional Pseudo-class: :is()"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The :is() pseudo-class takes a selector list as its argument and selects any element that can be selected by one of the selectors in that list. This helps prevent massive, repetitive selector lists."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"/* Traditional Approach */
header h1, main h1, footer h1 {
  color: #333;
}

/* Using :is() */
:is(header, main, footer) h1 {
  color: #333;
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Zero Specificity: :where()"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The :where() pseudo-class works identically to :is() in terms of grouping selectors, but with one major difference: its specificity is always zero. This makes it exceptionally useful for creating resilient base styles and design systems."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"/* Using :where() allows easy overriding later without extra specificity */
:where(article, section) p {
  margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
}

/* This simple selector easily overrides the rule above */
p {
  margin-bottom: 1rem;
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"The Relational Pseudo-class: :has()"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Often referred to as the 'parent selector', :has() lets you check if an element contains specific descendants or matches certain conditions relative to itself. This bridges a long-standing gap in CSS functionality."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"/* Style an article differently if it contains an image */
article:has(img) {
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
  padding: 1rem;
}

/* Style a form field container when an input inside is invalid */
.form-group:has(input:invalid) {
  border-color: red;
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Takeaways"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use :is() to reduce repetition when applying the same styles to grouped selectors."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use :where() when you need grouping functionality but want to maintain zero specificity for easy overrides."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use :has() to style parent or ancestor elements based on their children and structural state."}]
